        <body>
            <div xml:id="d665">

                <pb facs="0925" n="819" sameAs="frus:frus1906p1/pg819"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Minister Jackson to
                        the Secretary of State.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >American Legation</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Athens</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1906-07-03"
                                ><hi rendition="#italic">July 3, 1906</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 414.—Greek Series.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to previous correspondence, I
                    have the honor to report that formal verbal representations were made conjointly
                    this afternoon by the representatives of the (six) powers signatory to the
                    treaty of Berlin, calling upon the Greek Government to exert itself more
                    effectively to prevent the formation of Greek bands, etc., and to apply itself
                    toward exercising a quieting influence upon the Greeks in Macedonia. I am not
                    informed as to the text of the “verbal” representations—a copy of which was left
                    with Mr. Skouses, the Greek minister of foreign affairs—but I understand that
                    they contained no direct reference to Roumania or the Koutzo-Wallachs. Mr.
                    Skouses replied at length to the foreign representatives, but he is said to have
                    made no new statement or argument, or to have made any promises.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">John B. Jackson.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
